<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sofia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Serdica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sofia is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, at the foot of Mount Vitosha. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a> it was a Thracian and then Roman city, where Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> issued an edict of toleration for Christians in 311. Taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a> in 809, it was a Bulgarian, Byzantine and from 1382 Ottoman city, and it was chosen as the capital of the new Bulgarian state in 1879.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sofia.jpg" alt="Sofia" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Deensel · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panoramic_view_over_central_Sofia_and_the_Vitosha_Mountain_2017-10-08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sofia lies in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in the Sofia Valley, at the northern foot of the Vitosha mountain, on an average altitude of 550 metres. It is surrounded by mountains: Vitosha to the south, Lyulin to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a> to the north. It is built west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskar">Iskar</a>. Its development as a settlement owes much to its central position in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>: three mountain passes lead to the city and have been key roads since antiquity, and Vitosha is the watershed between the Black and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> seas. Hot water springs still flow abundantly in the centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has a history of nearly 7,000 years. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> village at Slatina is documented. The earliest tribes were the Thracian Tilataei, and in the 500s BCE the area joined the Odrysian kingdom. In 339 BCE Philip II of Macedon destroyed and ravaged the town for the first time. The Celtic tribe Serdi gave the city its name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a>; the earliest mention is an Athenian inscription of the 1st century BCE, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Dio Cassius</a> the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> subdued the Serdi. The emperor Marcus Ulpius Traianus gave it the name Ulpia Serdica.</p><p>During the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> the city was raided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. In 809 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Khan Krum</a> incorporated it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, and it became known as Sredets. In 1018 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> ended Bulgarian rule until 1194, when the Second Bulgarian Empire took it back. Sredets was a major administrative, economic, cultural and literary hub until the Ottoman conquest in 1382. From 1530 to 1836 Sofia was the regional capital of Rumelia Eyalet, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>'s largest and most important province.</p><p>Its population fell from 70,000 in the late 18th century to 19,000 in 1870 and 11,649 in 1878. Bulgarian rule was restored in 1878, and Sofia was selected as the capital of the Third Bulgarian State the next year, beginning a period of intense demographic and economic growth. After the Liberation War, knyaz Alexander Battenberg invited architects from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> to shape the new capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sofia is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Ancient Roman, Byzantine and medieval Bulgarian buildings survive in the centre, among them the 4th-century Rotunda of St. George, the walls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a> fortress and part of the Amphitheatre of Serdica. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyana-church">Boyana Church</a> was included on the World Heritage List in 1979.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sofia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Romania stayed out of the first Balkan war but entered the Second Balkan War in June 1913 against Bulgaria: 330,000 troops crossed the Danube, one army occupying Southern Dobruja and another threatening <b>Sofia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-romania">Kingdom of Romania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal and noble patronage produced the Boyana Church frescoes of 1259 near <b>Sofia</b>, the illuminated Bulgarian translation of the Manasses Chronicle and the Tetraevangelion of Ivan Alexander of 1355–1356, now in the British Library.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…the remodelled Hagia Irene, and in modern times the Kronstadt Naval Cathedral reproduces it almost exactly in size and geometry, while churches from <b>Sofia</b> and London to St. Louis and São Paulo, and synagogues such as the Hurva Synagogue and the Great Synagogue of Florence, drew on it.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Russo-Turkish War, Russian forces under General Gurko entered <b>Sofia</b>, ending nearly five centuries of Ottoman rule.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-4">4 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The battle near Slivnitsa, west of <b>Sofia</b>, which began on 17 November, ended with the Bulgarian army under Prince Alexander of Battenberg defeating the invading Serbian army of King Milan I. The victory secured Bulgaria's union with Eastern Rumelia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-19">19 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1879 the Tarnovo Constitution was adopted and Alexander of Battenberg elected prince, with <b>Sofia</b> as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bulgaria">Principality of Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
